# ESA’s new Sentinel data Zarr format for flood analysis 

### Temporal analysis and monitoring routines using Sentinel-1 data are about to become easier thanks to the new Zarr format. We’ll see how we can create workflows for basic SAR operations using cloud-native tools to study the 2024 Valencia floods.

## ESA EOPF family
I started my summer internship at Development Seed, working on the [EOPF toolkit project](https://github.com/eopf-toolkit). From an outside perspective, I wanted to demonstrate how to use, work and play with the Sentinel-1 data using the new `.zarr` format. 

My entering gate (and main helper) was the [EOPF 101](https://github.com/eopf-toolkit/eopf-101/tree/main). This toolkit is a facilitator for early `.zarr` users (just like me!), helping navigating through and understanding this cloud based format. 

In the end, to apply everything I learned, I created a use case with Sentinel-1 GRD Zarr data, focusing on monitoring the 2024 Valencia floods. But we’ll get to it!

## But why `.zarr` matters?
The `.zarr` format is cloud-native, chunked, compressed and organized in n-dimension arrays. But these are just words.. What does this really mean? 

Instead of downloading and organizing huge datasets, we can store very large datasets and access only the relevant data we need. The rest of the dataset is left **on-the-fly**. Using the Sentinel-1 GRD example: if we only need to access the measurement group for its variables, why would we load everything else? We don’t! We manipulate the data access to meet our needs.


