A good strategy for Sentinel-1 GRD flood monitoring is to use time series analysis. Since this requires large amounts of data repeated over time, storing datasets in the `.zarr` format helps overcome the complexity of downloading and opening files. It allows us to load only the data we want and access just the variables we need.

## Valencia floods analysis meets cloud-optimized format
With the little we knew about `.zarr`, we applied it to a real case study: the massive [2024 Valencia floods](https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/apr/15/europe-storms-floods-and-wildfires-in-2024-affected-more-than-400000). This turned out to be the perfect performance test, since it involved not just one dataset but **14**! Normally, this would mean downloading 14 `.SAFE` files but with `.zarr` no downloads were needed. 

We manipulated the data directly and accessed only the variables required for our SAR operations. The `xarray` library was a key component. `zarray`’s lazy loading capability ensures that only the necessary data is loaded into memory. The actual computation only happens when we explicitly request results, like plotting or interpolating coordinates. Libraries like `xarray-sentinel` also proved valuable, particularly for intensity backscatter computation.

Because we wanted to run a time series analysis, we stacked the datasets along a new time dimension. We  took advantage of the cool `zarr` and `xarray` combination. To be fair, this was a challenging part:

1. To stack several datasets into a single one they must share the same dimensions, shapes and dimension values.
2. Sentinel-1 GRD data does not naturally behave this way. It is expectable to have the same dimensions but not the same shape and most definitely not the same dimension values.
3. [Coregistration](https://elisecolin.medium.com/what-is-coregistration-in-remote-sensing-7d76d48d337d) is the usual solution for aligning SAR images, but currently no python library supports coregistration of `zarr` data.

But `.zarr` is so easy to handle and manipulate that we were able to perform a DIY coregistration using only xarray.

