Not all tools are available yet and some SAR operations still can’t be done as they were in some other SAR processing applications, such as SNAP (ESA's free software platform for processing and analyzing Sentinel data - it performs all the necessary operation over SAR data). But with `.zarr` the processing was incredibly fast. What would have been slow and heavy in SNAP, ran smoothly on a full python environment. And the best part was that we handled everything in one place without switching softwares.

## Rocks on the way? Let's build a castle then!
There’s still some challenges we need to face regarding Sentinel-1 `.zarr`:
- For Sentinel-1 GRD data, most datasets are not (**yet!**) available in the STAC catalog where `.SAFE` data is converted to `.zarr`. But they will be! Currently, this limitation makes it harder to work with, manage or even play Sentinel-1 data. For last minute needs, there are two useful emergency contacts: 
    1. Requesting specific data on [EOPF sample data request](https://github.com/EOPF-Sample-Service/eopf-sample-data). 
    2. [Convert the data](https://gitlab.eopf.copernicus.eu/cpm/eopf-cpm) yourself, which is not that hard (trust me!).
- There are only a few Python libraries that handle SAR operations for `.zarr`-based data. Within these small groups, the available operations are limited. However, intensity backscatter computation is possible, which has already solved many challenges in our flood mapping workflow.

